(6159784, 907791, 83313, '<td valign="top">I''m reviewing this challenge a second time after considering what the s-curve technique is really supposed to be. That is a compositional technique used to lead the viewer into the image. I''m not really sure where the use of the s-curve is in this, though I think you did see it. I''m dinging it some, but not by much.</td>', 'I''m reviewing this challenge a second time after considering what the s-curve technique is really supposed to be. That is a compositional technique used to lead the viewer into the image. I''m not really sure where the use of the s-curve is in this, though I think you did see it. I''m dinging it some, but not by much.', '2010-09-12 14:54:40.000000', NULL, 1),
(6159787, 907880, 83313, '<td valign="top">I''m reviewing this challenge a second time after considering what the s-curve technique is really supposed to be. That is a compositional technique used to lead the viewer into the image. Though there is an obvious s-curve in the cello, I don''t find it to be an eye leading element that takes me into or through the image.</td>', 'I''m reviewing this challenge a second time after considering what the s-curve technique is really supposed to be. That is a compositional technique used to lead the viewer into the image. Though there is an obvious s-curve in the cello, I don''t find it to be an eye leading element that takes me into or through the image.', '2010-09-12 14:56:19.000000', NULL, 1),
(6159788, 907455, 83313, '<td valign="top">I''m reviewing this challenge a second time after considering what the s-curve technique is really supposed to be. That is a compositional technique used to lead the viewer into the image. Though there is an obvious s-curve in the arrangement of the glasses, I''m personally not finding it to be an element that leads my eye into or through the scene. I''m giving this one the benefit of the doubt, however and letting my earlier vote stand.</td>', 'I''m reviewing this challenge a second time after considering what the s-curve technique is really supposed to be. That is a compositional technique used to lead the viewer into the image. Though there is an obvious s-curve in the arrangement of the glasses, I''m personally not finding it to be an element that leads my eye into or through the scene. I''m giving this one the benefit of the doubt, however and letting my earlier vote stand.', '2010-09-12 14:59:09.000000', NULL, 1),
